   8.185	{"model":"llama3.2:1b","response":"The history of the printing press is a fascinating story that spans over five centuries, from the 15th to the 20th century. It was a revolutionary invention that transformed the way people communicated, learned, and created art. In this essay, we will delve into the history of the printing press, exploring its development, evolution, and impact on society.\n\nThe earliest known predecessor to the printing press was the \"Auricularium,\" a wooden or stone box used in ancient Rome to store and print manuscripts. The box was invented by a Roman architect named Eusebius, who designed it in the late 1st century AD. The Auricularium was a simple box with a lid, used for storing and printing books, manuscripts, and other documents.\n\nHowever, it was not until the 15th century that the printing press as we know it today began to take shape. In 1450, Johannes Gutenberg, a German goldsmith and printer, invented the first movable-type printing press. The press was a combination of a type of wooden frame, metal characters, and movable metal type pieces. The movable type allowed the printer to change the characters in the middle of a line of text, making it possible to print large quantities of books quickly and accurately.\n\nGutenberg's innovation was to use a combination of wood and metal to create the movable-type press. The wooden frame was made of oak, which was chosen for its strength and durability. The metal characters were made of brass, which was chosen for its high melting point and durability. The movable metal type pieces were made of tin, which was chosen for its high melting point and durability.\n\nThe first printed book published by Gutenberg was the \"Gutenberg Bible,\" which consisted of 1,286 pages of text and 1,245 illustrations. The book was printed on high-quality paper made from rye flour and was bound in two covers. The Gutenberg Bible was a groundbreaking achievement, as it was the first book to use the movable-type printing press. It also marked the beginning of the end of the traditional manuscript-based culture, as it paved the way for the mass production of books.\n\nHowever, it was not until the 1470s that the printing press became widely available. The first books to be printed using the movable-type press were religious texts, such as the Bible and the Psalms. The printing press was also used to print books on medicine, philosophy, and other subjects.\n\nThe printing press gained popularity in Europe in the 15th century, particularly in Germany, Italy","context":[128000,128006,9125,128007,271,38766,1303,33025,2696,25,6790,220,2366,18,198,15724,2696,25,220,1591,17907,220,2366,21,271,128009,128006,882,128007,271,8144,264,11944,9071,315,520,3325,220,1049,15,4339,389,279,3925,315,279,18991,3577,13,128009,128006,78191,128007,271,791,3925,315,279,18991,3577,374,264,27387,3446,430,45395,927,4330,24552,11,505,279,220,868,339,311,279,220,508,339,9478,13,1102,574,264,30191,28229,430,24411,279,1648,1274,59035,11,9687,11,323,3549,1989,13,763,420,9071,11,584,690,82845,1139,279,3925,315,279,18991,3577,11,24919,1202,4500,11,15740,11,323,5536,389,8396,382,791,30758,3967,40274,311,279,18991,3577,574,279,330,32,324,24553,2411,1359,264,23162,477,9998,3830,1511,304,14154,22463,311,3637,323,1194,79688,13,578,3830,574,36592,555,264,13041,11726,7086,469,817,8385,355,11,889,6319,433,304,279,3389,220,16,267,9478,9827,13,578,35731,24553,2411,574,264,4382,3830,449,264,27431,11,1511,369,28672,323,18991,6603,11,79688,11,323,1023,9477,382,11458,11,433,574,539,3156,279,220,868,339,9478,430,279,18991,3577,439,584,1440,433,3432,6137,311,1935,6211,13,763,220,9591,15,11,55205,52686,11,264,6063,6761,34117,323,23185,11,36592,279,1176,83679,10827,18991,3577,13,578,3577,574,264,10824,315,264,955,315,23162,4124,11,9501,5885,11,323,83679,9501,955,9863,13,578,83679,955,5535,279,23185,311,2349,279,5885,304,279,6278,315,264,1584,315,1495,11,3339,433,3284,311,1194,3544,33776,315,6603,6288,323,30357,382,38,